- op: discuss  # 6263
  at: '2026-06-02T04:53:06Z'  # 6263
  by: agent  # 6263
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6263
  clock: 6  # 6263
  nonce: '6263'  # 6263
  message: "**Pass-24 substrate-verified faces (F24.1, F24.7) on the AR.pass and edge.meta.evidence_type axes.**\n\n**(F24.1) Linker pass_id naming convention is inconsistent.** 36 linker passes carry `-linker` suffix; 5 linker-class passes (Tier 2 edge-recovery per ADR-0003-ext) don't: `decorator-dispatch`, `di-resolution`, `inherited-calls`, `method-call-recovery`, `type-hierarchy`. A consumer iterating `analysis_runs[].pass` and filtering by suffix-pattern misses these 5. The `generate-architecture` script enforces module docstring subcategory convention but doesn't gate pass_id naming. INV-numat (\"Vocabulary fields on edges and edge metadata mix multiple semantic axes\") at the AR.pass axis.\n\n**(F24.7) `evidence_type` axis has two naming conventions.** 33 distinct evidence_type values split into:\n\n- **`ast_*` prefix** (~10 values): `ast_call_direct, ast_call, ast_import, ast_new, ast_call_method, ast_name_read, ast_decorator, ast_extends, ast_implements, ast_method_this, ast_method_inferred, ast_type_ref` — AST-derived evidence. ~96% of edges by volume (78,166 / 109,915).\n- **No prefix / different convention** (~23 values): `naming_convention, span_overlap, function_reference, module_attribute_reference, type_hierarchy, enclosing_scope, topic_match, module_export_heuristic, import_to_manifest, variable_match, import_static, import_resolution, static, cffi_stdlib_call, ctypes_stdlib_call, import_declaration, trait_impl, use_declaration` — heuristic / derived / linker-emitted evidence.\n\nThe axis carries two sub-categories — direct-AST vs derived-evidence — but the value namespace doesn't surface the distinction. A consumer filtering on \"show me only AST-derived edges\" must hard-code the 10 ast_* values rather than query a sub-axis tag. ADR-0028 (Edge.evidence_type axis per ADR-0024 §\"Fold-residue discipline\") doesn't address this; possible follow-up is to declare a typed-sibling `evidence_class: 'ast_derived' | 'heuristic' | 'linker_inferred'` matching the ADR-0031 / ADR-0032 pattern.\n\nBoth faces are discussion-only observations; no new tracker items filed. Future structural-fix work would naturally bundle them with the ADR-0031/0032 typed-sibling pattern reapplied to AR.pass and edge.evidence_type.\n\n**Lab notebook entry:** `~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/notebookjournal_06022026_0250_pass24.md`."  # 6263
- op: discuss  # 42b0
  at: '2026-06-02T05:21:42Z'  # 42b0
  by: agent  # 42b0
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 42b0
  clock: 7  # 42b0
  nonce: 42b0  # 42b0
  message: "Pass-25 cross-format consistency probe — ADR-0031 migration broadly incomplete on producer side. Substrate /tmp/hg-dogfood-s21/bm.json:\n\nF25.12: `discovery_language` is populated on <1% of nodes (~258/34253):\n- call_site: 100% (130/130) — emitted by linkers\n- enum: 100% (5/5); interface: 85.7% (6/7); package: 66.7% (2/3)\n- function: 1.8% (128/7098)\n- method/class/file/external_symbol/variable/dependency/export/project/struct/getter: 0%\n\nFor all 11 language analyzers emitting native-kind nodes (python/bash/javascript/java/go/rust/swift/html/csharp/solidity/yaml_ansible), 0 nodes have discovery_language populated — they still emit the legacy `language` field. Q30 cross-check: 0 nodes have BOTH `language` and `discovery_language` populated; the two fields are mutually exclusive on every node. ADR-0031 split shipped at the schema layer but the producer migration is mostly absent — language analyzers were not migrated to set `discovery_language`. Only the linker/orchestrator emits the new axis (on call_site, enum, package, interface — synthetic and namespace kinds).\n\nCross-format pair tested: `AR.pass` vs dominant `node.discovery_language` for AR's origin_run_id cohort — 0/11 matches across non-linker ARs (dominant value is None for every analyzer's node cohort).\n\nF25.17 (minor secondary): profile.frameworks=['lit','starlette'] / dev_frameworks=['pytest'] declare detected frameworks but 0 nodes reference 'lit'/'starlette'/'pytest' in any of (language, discovery_language, protocol_origin). Frameworks have no node-level provenance link.\n\nThe \"one axis populated per node, two axes serialized per node\" pattern fits INV-numat's existing scope (vocabulary fields mix axes). Migration-completion question: is `discovery_language` meant to replace `language` for native kinds, or only supplement it for synthetic kinds? ADR-0031 re-reading needed to call this design-intentional vs unfinished."  # 42b0
- op: discuss  # 709b
  at: '2026-06-02T06:13:58Z'  # 709b
  by: agent  # 709b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 709b
  clock: 8  # 709b
  nonce: 709b  # 709b
  message: "Pass-27 INV-numat two faces — entrypoint.kind axis mixing + entrypoint surface lacks id while sibling surfaces have one.\n\n**Face 1: entrypoint.kind mixes symbol-nature kinds with file-role kinds.**\n\nPass-27 cross-tabulated `ep.kind` against `node.kind` for resolved entrypoints (104/104 resolve):\n\n| ep.kind | node.kind it resolves to | count |\n|---|---|---|\n| shell_script | file | 36 |\n| library_export | function | 32 |\n| library_export | class | 14 |\n| main_function | function | 9 |\n| main_function | file | 2 |\n| http_route | function | 8 |\n| script_module | file | 2 |\n| html_entry | file | 1 |\n\n`ep.kind='shell_script'` always resolves to file-kind nodes (file-role kind), `ep.kind='library_export'` always resolves to function-or-class (symbol-nature kind), `ep.kind='main_function'` resolves mostly to function-kind but 2/11 resolve to FILE-kind — the inconsistency is the axis-mixing tell. The `main_function` kind is documented in spec §8 (\"Entry point type\") as semantically about a function (the main() routine), but at runtime it's stamped on file-kind Symbols too. Either the detection rule is right and the kind label should be `script_module` or `main_entry` for the file-kind case, or the file-kind cases are mis-stamping.\n\nThis is the third application of the field-axis-mixing pattern after ADR-0031 / ADR-0032 / WI-kadop (Symbol.origin) / WI-didar (edge.meta.call_construct). The entrypoint.kind axis is documented in spec §8 (\"Entry point type (http_route, cli_command, main_function, background_task, websocket_handler, library_export, shell_script, html_entry, script_module, connectivity_based, ...)\") — the documentation itself mixes axes: file-role kinds (`shell_script`, `html_entry`, `script_module`) coexist with symbol-nature kinds (`main_function`, `http_route`, `library_export`) without distinguishing them.\n\n**Face 2: entrypoint records lack an `id` field while sibling top-level surfaces all have one.**\n\nTop-level record-id discipline:\n- features: every record has `id` (sha256-hashed)\n- usage_contexts: every record has `id` (usage:sha256-hashed)\n- entrypoints: ABSENT — no record has an id field\n\nConsumers wanting to cross-reference an entrypoint by stable ID cannot; they must use `entrypoint.symbol_id` (which is the resolved node.id, not an entrypoint-level identity). This means an entrypoint cannot be referenced as itself — only via the node it points to — so two entrypoints on the same symbol with different kinds (the documented pattern when a symbol qualifies as both http_route AND library_export) would be indistinguishable by reference.\n\nThe same family as INV-kurup's identifier-prefix divergence but expressed as an absence rather than a divergence.\n\nProbe artifacts: /tmp/pass27_probe1.py + /tmp/pass27_probe5.py / outputs at /tmp/pass27_output[1,5].log.\n\nNo status change; pass-27 enriches INV-numat's face catalog."  # 709b
- op: discuss  # 261a
  at: '2026-06-02T07:09:06Z'  # 261a
  by: agent  # 261a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 261a
  clock: 9  # 261a
  nonce: 261a  # 261a
  message: "Pass-30 axis-vocabulary divergence findings — 2 sub-faces (F30.4 ep.kind ↔ websocket_handler absent / WS folded into http_route; F30.9 ep.kind ↔ meta.concepts.concept axis mismatch).\n\n**F30.4 — entrypoint.kind absent kinds for documented vocabulary.** Spec §9.882 lists 10 entrypoint kinds: http_route, cli_command, main_function, background_task, websocket_handler, library_export, shell_script, html_entry, script_module, connectivity_based. Substrate exhibits 6: library_export (46), shell_script (36), main_function (11), http_route (8), script_module (2), html_entry (1). **4 documented kinds absent: cli_command, background_task, websocket_handler, connectivity_based.**\n\nThree of the 4 absences may be codebase-conditional (this codebase has no CLI/background/connectivity patterns). But WebSocket endpoints DO exist in the codebase (`hypergumbo-tracker/serve.py:215 _ws_handler`) — they are filed under `kind='http_route'` with `meta.method='WS'` and `label='HTTP WS /ws'`. Producer-design diverges from spec narrative; the `websocket_handler` kind is reserved-but-never-emitted while WS endpoints are silently classified as `http_route`.\n\nSub-pattern (1) \"same-fact-two-names\" applied to the entrypoint-kind axis: spec calls one thing `websocket_handler`, substrate calls the same thing `http_route` with `meta.method='WS'`. Spec-drift OR producer-coarsening-choice depending on perspective.\n\n**F30.9 — entrypoint.kind vs meta.concepts.concept axis mismatch on 10/102 cases.** 92/102 entrypoints (90.2%) have ep.kind matching a concept name in their target node's meta.concepts.concept set. Misaligned pairs:\n\n- **8 http_route ↔ concept='route'** — axis-vocabulary divergence (entrypoint axis uses http_route; concepts axis uses route, with HTTP method in `method` sub-field)\n- **2 main_function ↔ concept='main_guard'** — axis-coarsening loss (concepts axis distinguishes `main_function` (def main(): ...) from `main_guard` (if __name__ == '__main__': ...); entrypoint axis folds both into `main_function`, dropping the distinction)\n\nThis is two independent axes for the same semantic fact. spec §8.590 defines meta.concepts shape (`{concept, framework, ...}`); spec §9.882 defines entrypoint.kind vocabulary (10 values). Neither says the two vocabularies should align, but consumer-side joining entrypoints to concepts requires this alignment. Pattern: axis-cluster vocabulary drift across two surfaces of the same fact.\n\n**Sub-pattern (1) at scale.** Existing catalog entry \"same-fact-two-names\" was named for AR.execution_id↔Symbol.origin_run_id and similar. Pass-30 F30.4 + F30.9 surface the same pattern at the producer-consumer axis layer: entrypoint detector and meta.concepts producer both classify the same semantic feature, but with different vocabulary registers. Adds 3 instances:\n- websocket_handler (spec ep axis) ↔ http_route + meta.method=WS (substrate ep axis)\n- http_route (ep axis) ↔ route (concepts axis)\n- main_function (ep axis) ↔ main_guard or main_function (concepts axis)\n\nConcretely: a consumer querying \"is this node an entrypoint?\" via meta.concepts.concept='route' will MATCH 8 http_route entrypoints; the same consumer querying via entrypoint.kind='websocket_handler' will MISS the 1 WS endpoint. The two indexes are not symmetric, even though they encode the same fact set.\n\nINV-numat covers vocabulary-mixing on edges; this is the same root pattern on nodes/entrypoints. Same finding-family as INV-numat's catalogued faces (cross_language meta, call_construct mixing method/cross_file, etc.).\n\nNo status change. Pass-30 supplies the producer-side axis-mismatch finding for the entrypoint↔concepts pair.\n\nProbe artifact: /tmp/pass30_probe2c.py (entrypoint.kind ↔ meta.concepts.concept) + /tmp/pass30_probe1.py probe 1.D (entrypoint kinds vocab)."  # 261a
- op: discuss  # c198
  at: '2026-06-02T07:33:47Z'  # c198
  by: agent  # c198
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c198
  clock: 10  # c198
  nonce: c198  # c198
  message: "Pass-31 face: verify-claims input vs output field-name asymmetry (`text` in YAML input vs `claim_text` in JSON output) — F31.5.\n\nSub-pattern (1) same-fact-two-names applied to verify-claims wire interface across input/output schemas.\n\n**Observation.** YAML input field per `verify_claims.py:188-228 load_claims()`:\n```python\nclaim = Claim(\n    id=entry.get(\"id\", \"\"),\n    text=entry.get(\"text\", \"\"),    # <-- input field name: 'text'\n    constraint_boundary=constraint.get(\"boundary\", \"\"),\n    ...\n)\n```\n\nJSON output field per `ClaimVerdict` dataclass (queried via `--json` output):\n```json\n{\n  \"claim_id\": \"must_have_python\",\n  \"claim_text\": \"At least 5 Python files exist\",    // <-- output field name: 'claim_text'\n  \"verdict\": \"inconclusive\",\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nSame content (the human-readable claim description), two field names depending on whether you read or write. A consumer round-tripping (read JSON verdicts, generate new claim YAML based on them) has to translate `claim_text` → `text`. The output field is prefixed with `claim_` while the input field isn't.\n\nCousin patterns:\n- Pass-30 F30.15: `--input` vs `--claims` flag-name divergence across subcommands\n- Pass-26 F26.15: `pass_id` (dataclass) → `pass` (JSON) AND `execution_id` (AR producer) → `origin_run_id` (consumer FK), both documented intentional\n- Pass-25 F25.13: `language_pack:*` (AR.toolchain.grammar_module) vs `tree-sitter-*` (reproducibility_context.captured.grammars)\n\nPattern: producer-side and consumer-side identity-field names diverge consistently across the hypergumbo schema. The verify-claims text/claim_text divergence is the smallest-scope instance (single value, single subcommand) but exhibits the same pattern at scale.\n\nFix-path candidate: canonicalize on either `text` or `claim_text` for both input AND output. Or document the asymmetry in spec.md / `verify-claims --help` so consumers know to translate.\n\nINV-numat covers vocabulary-mixing on edges; this is the same root pattern on subcommand wire interface. Same finding-family as INV-numat's catalogued faces.\n\nNo status change. Pass-31 supplies the verify-claims text/claim_text divergence to INV-numat's catalog.\n\nProbe artifact: pass-31 probe C — verify_claims.py:218 `text=entry.get(\"text\", \"\")` vs JSON output `claim_text` field."  # c198
- op: discuss  # 7116
  at: '2026-06-02T17:13:01Z'  # 7116
  by: agent  # 7116
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7116
  clock: 11  # 7116
  nonce: '7116'  # 7116
  message: "Pass-34 batch (2 findings — vocabulary axis at cross-surface reference fields + entrypoint-kind/feature-scope asymmetry).\n\n**F34.B2 — Three top-level surfaces, three different field names for \"what node does this reference\":**\n- `entrypoint.symbol_id` (string, sha256-style node ID, 104 records)\n- `feature.entry_nodes` (list of node ID strings, 16 records × 1 entry)\n- `usage_context.symbol_ref` (string, full identifier path `lang:path:span:name:kind`, 8 records)\n\nUC record introspected directly (`/tmp/p34_outputB2.log` B2.3-B2.4):\n```\nkeys: ['context_name', 'id', 'kind', 'metadata', 'path', 'position', 'span', 'symbol_ref']\nsymbol_ref: 'python:packages/hypergumbo-tracker/src/hypergumbo_tracker/serve.py:101-107:_health:function'\n```\nNo `symbol_id` field on UC. The format of UC.symbol_ref matches the format of `node.id` AND the format of feature.entry_nodes[i] — they're all the canonical Symbol identifier — but the field name differs at each surface.\n\nSub-pattern (1) \"same-fact-multiple-names\" at the cross-surface reference axis. The three names encode the same concept (a node reference); pass-21 F6 cataloged AR.execution_id vs Symbol.origin_run_id (AR side); pass-25 F25.14 cataloged AR.pass vs pass_id (conceptual alias); F34.B2 adds three-way divergence at the entrypoint/feature/UC reference axis.\n\nCousin pattern to pass-27 F27.4 (entrypoint records lack an `id` field while sibling features/UCs have ones). F27.4 covered the entry-record's primary identity; F34.B2 covers the reference-to-other-node field. Both are axis-naming-uniformity gaps at the top-level surface layer.\n\n**F34.B1 — features cover ONLY http_route entrypoint kind; 96 of 104 entrypoints (92.3%) have no corresponding feature.**\n\nPer-kind breakdown of the 96 EPs not in feature.entry_nodes (`/tmp/p34_outputB2.log` B2.2):\n- library_export: 46 (Python public-API exports — TrackerSet, migrate, Cache, validate_all, ...)\n- shell_script: 36 (each .githooks/* file)\n- main_function: 11 (Python main() functions + `if __name__ == '__main__'` modules)\n- script_module: 2\n- html_entry: 1\n\nPer-kind breakdown of the 8 EPs IN features: all 8 are http_route (kind:Counter == {'http_route': 8}).\n\nComplement to pass-33 F33.18 (\"all 16 features are from hypergumbo-tracker/serve.py\"): F34.B1 quantifies the asymmetry from the entrypoint side. The \"feature\" surface is effectively a one-kind projection of entrypoints. 5 other entrypoint kinds (library_export, shell_script, main_function, script_module, html_entry) have NO feature counterpart. This is a substrate-narrowing decision encoded in the feature detection code but not documented in spec §13 (\"Features\" implies named slices of the codebase, not \"HTTP routes only\").\n\n**Fix-path candidate.** Either:\n(a) Extend feature detection to non-http-route entrypoint kinds (each library_export could be a \"library API feature\"; each main_function could be a \"CLI entrypoint feature\").\n(b) Rename `features` → `http_features` or `route_features` to match the actual scope.\n(c) Document at spec §13 that features = http_route entrypoints with edge_id/node_id projections.\n\n**Why on INV-numat.** Sub-pattern (1) is the existing INV-numat scope (vocabulary fields mix axes). F34.B2 is a 3-surface application; F34.B1 is the asymmetric-scope manifestation at the entrypoint→feature pair. Both fit \"field axis carries unintended subset/scope information beyond its declared semantics.\"\n\n**Probe artifacts:** `/tmp/p34_outputB.log`, `/tmp/p34_outputB2.log`, `/tmp/p34_probeB.py`, `/tmp/p34_probeB2.py`. No status change."  # 7116
- op: discuss  # cb98
  at: '2026-06-02T17:33:34Z'  # cb98
  by: agent  # cb98
  actor: jgstern_agent  # cb98
  clock: 12  # cb98
  nonce: cb98  # cb98
  message: "Pass-35 batch (2 findings — feature-vs-EP coverage ratio + EP-kind diversity both codebase-shape-dependent).\n\n**F35.C2 — Feature/EP coverage ratio depends entirely on per-codebase EP-kind mix.** Cross-substrate comparison:\n- ROOT substrate: 8/104 = 7.7% of EPs become features\n- TRACKER substrate: 8/15 = 53.3% of EPs become features\n- CLI_PKG substrate: 0/1 = 0% of EPs become features\n\nPass-33 F33.18 said \"all 16 features from one module\" (root). Pass-34 F34.B1 said \"96/104 EPs (92.3%) have no feature\" (root). Pass-35 cross-substrate finding: the feature emission gate is \"EP[kind=http_route] → feature\" in ALL substrates. The relative coverage RATIO depends on codebase content:\n- Tracker has 8/15 = 53.3% http_route EPs → high feature coverage\n- Root has 8/104 = 7.7% http_route EPs → low feature coverage  \n- CLI_PKG has 0/1 = 0% http_route EPs → no features at all\n\nThe substrate-narrowing \"feature = http_route-only projection\" is structural; the appearance of feature coverage is codebase-shape artifact. Documentation at spec §13 (\"features = named slices of the codebase\") implies broader scope; producer reality is narrower.\n\n**F35.C3 — EP kind diversity is codebase-shape-dependent.** Per-substrate EP kind counts:\n```\nROOT:    6 kinds  {main_function, http_route, shell_script, script_module, html_entry, library_export}\nTRACKER: 3 kinds  {main_function, http_route, library_export}\nCLI_PKG: 1 kind   {main_function}\n```\nTRACKER lacks shell_script (no .githooks/scripts), script_module (no __main__.py outside serve module), html_entry (no .html in pkg scope). CLI_PKG further lacks http_route and library_export. The EP-kind detection vocabulary is universal but per-substrate manifestation depends on file presence. Consumers writing kind-conditional logic against entrypoint.kind axis must account for codebase-shape thinning of the kind set.\n\nCousin to F35.B2 (INV-bikaj — 64 ARs fire on 32-node cli_pkg with most emitting nothing): same root mechanism — kind-conditional activation gates don't shape-prune based on codebase content.\n\n**Why on INV-numat.** INV-numat covers vocabulary axes mixing/scope. F35.C2 covers the feature-axis-projection-vs-documented-scope gap; F35.C3 covers the kind-axis-vocabulary-vs-substrate-manifestation gap. Both fit the \"axis carries unintended scope/subset information beyond declared semantics\" pattern.\n\n**Probe artifacts:** `/tmp/p35_outputB.log` B.4 + B.5 + B.12 cross-codebase tables; `/tmp/p35_outputC.log` C.4 + C.5 + C.7 tracker-pkg specifics. No status change."  # cb98
